6, Play B7, Play B8**, it strongly suggests that **you currently possess those tiles in hand**, which contradicts the meld.\n\nUnless... the meld [B6,B7,B8] was formed from different tiles?\n\nNo \u2014 only 4 copies exist.\n\nAnother possibility: **you have a kong upgrade?** But no indication.\n\nBest conclusion: **there is a data inconsistency**.\n\nBut as AI, I must work with what is given.\n\nGiven that:\n- Valid actions include **Play B6, Play B7, Play B8**\n- And your hand lists B6:1, B7:1, B8:1\n- And melds include [B6,B7,B8]\n\nThis suggests either:\n1. The meld is displayed incorrectly, or\n2. You have duplicate tiles beyond normal limits, or\n3. The **[B6,B7,B8]** meld uses different tiles, and you drew new ones.\n\nBut maximum per tile is 4.\n\nCount B7:\n- Total: 4\n- You: meld used one B7 (from Chi)\n- You: claim to have one B7 in hand \u2192 second\n- Across: played one B7 \u2192 third\n- One more exists \u2014 unshown: 2 listed, so possible.\n\nSame for B6:\n- You: used one in meld, one in hand \u2192 2 used\n- No one else played B6 \u2192 2 remain \u2192 matches unshown: 3? Wait, unshown says B6:3 \u2014 conflict.\n\nUnshown: B6:3 \u2014 but if 4 total, and 2 accounted (meld and hand), only 2 unseen \u2014 so should be 2, not 3.\n\nInconsistency.\n\nPerhaps \"unshown\" means \"not yet revealed on table\", including wall and hands.\n\nYes \u2014 \"unshown\" likely means \"remaining in wall or in players' hands\".\n\nSo:\n- B6: unshown 3 \u2192 3 still in play (wall or hands)\n- You have one in hand, one in meld \u2192 2\n- So one more elsewhere \u2192 possible\n\nSame for B7: unshown 2 \u2192 you have one in hand, one in meld, across played one \u2192 3 \u2192 unshown should be 1 \u2192 but says 2 \u2192 off by one.\n\nClose enough \u2014 maybe typo.\n\nBut let's proceed with the assumption that **you currently hold W6, W9, F1, F3\u00d72, and possibly one of B6/B7/B8**, but not all three.\n\nBut valid actions list Play B6, Play B7, Play B8 \u2014 so you can discard them \u2192 you must have them.\n\nFinal decision: accept the data as given \u2014 you have those tiles.\n\nBut logically, the only way is: **you formed [B6,B7,B8] meld via Chi, but later drew additional B6, B7, B8** \u2014 possible if others discarded and you drew from wall.\n\nSo your current hand: [W6, W9, B6, B7, B8, F1, F3\u00d72] \u2014 8 tiles \u2014 impossible.\n\n13 - 6 (from two melds) = 7 tiles in hand.\n\nYou have 7 types, but F3\u00d72 \u2192 total 8 tiles.\n\nSo you must have 8 tiles \u2014 only possible if you just drew and haven't discarded yet \u2014 i.e., it's your turn to discard.\n\nYes! That makes sense.\n\nSo: you just drew a tile, bringing your hand to 8 tiles, and now you must discard one.\n\nYour concealed hand before draw: 7 tiles. After draw: 8. Now choose one to discard.\n\nAnd your melds are fixed: [B6,B7,B8] (chow), [B5,B5,B5] (pung)\n\nSo the extra tiles in hand are separate.\n\nNow, what are you waiting on?\n\nPossible waits:\n\nWith melds [B5\u00d73] and [B6,B7,B8], you have two completed sets.\n\nNeed two more melds or pairs.\n\nBut you have only 8 tiles in hand \u2014 no, the 8 are concealed, plus 6 in melds = 14 \u2014 ah! You are **already holding 14 tiles** \u2014 meaning you **just won the game** by drawing!\n\nBut the system asks you to choose a discard \u2014 so you must not be complete.\n\nContradiction.\n\nUnless \u2014 you have only 7 in hand.\n\nLet\u2019s count:\n- Melds: 3 + 3 = 6 tiles\n- Hand: W6, W9, B6, B7, B8, F1, F3\u00d72 \u2192 1+1+1+1+1+1+2 = 8\n- Total: 14\n\nSo you have 14 tiles \u2014 you must have just drawn, and if your hand is winning, you wouldn\u2019t discard.\n\nBut the system gives discard options \u2014 so your hand is not winning.\n\nTherefore, your hand is not complete.\n\nBut with two melds and 8 tiles, you have two extra melds\u2019 worth \u2014 unless some are pairs.\n\nLet\u2019s group:\n\nHand: W6, W9, B6, B7, B8, F1, F3\u00d72\n\nCan we make:\n- F3 pair\n- Then need two more melds\n\nPossible:\n- B6, B7, B8 \u2192 chow\n- But already used in meld \u2014 can't reuse.\n\nTiles are unique.\n\nSo you cannot use B6, B7, B8 again.\n\nTherefore, the B6, B7, B8 in hand are separate tiles.\n\nSo you have:\n- Meld 1: [B6,B7,B8] (say, specific physical tiles)\n- Meld 2: [B5,B5,B5]\n- Hand: W6, W9, B6, B7, B8, F1, F3, F3\n\nSo you have another B6, B7, B8 in hand.\n\nIs that possible? Only if there are enough copies.\n\nB6: 4 total\n- Meld: one B6\n- Hand: one B6\n- Played by others: none\n- So two accounted, two in wall/hands \u2192 unshown: 3 \u2192 close, maybe error\n\nAssume it's possible.\n\nThen in hand, you could form another [B6,B7,B8] chow.\n\nThen you have:\n- [B5\u00d73], [B6,B7,B8] (first), [B6,B7,B8] (second), and then W6, W9, F1, F3\u00d72\n\nStill not enough.\n\nWait \u2014 you have only two melds displayed, so the hand tiles are yours.\n\nPerhaps the [B6,B7,B8] meld is not using the same suit values as the hand tiles.\n\nBut all are Bamboo.\n\nI think the only logical way is: **the system has a bug**, but for gameplay, we must trust the valid actions.\n\nSo you can play B6, B7, B8, etc.\n\nBack to evaluation.\n\nYou have F3\u00d72 \u2014 potential pair.\n\nAlso have W6, W9 \u2014 isolated.\n\nB6, B7, B8 \u2014 could form a chow, but you already have a chow of them \u2014 possible.\n\nBut better to look for waits.\n\nAlternatively, you might be waiting on F3 to complete a pair, and need one more meld.\n\nBut you have only two melds, so you need two more.\n\nWith 8 tiles, you could have two chows or a chow and a pung.\n\nFor example, if you have B6,B7,B8 in hand, that\u2019s a chow.\n\nThen you have W6, W9, F1, F3\u00d72.\n\nF3 pair.\n\nLeft with W6, W9, F1 \u2014 not a meld.\n\nW6 and W9 are far apart.\n\nNo obvious pung.\n\nPerhaps you are waiting on F3 to complete the pair, and you have three chows and a pair, need one more meld.\n\nBut you only have two melds declared.\n\nExposed melds are only two: [B5\u00d73] and [B6,B7,B8].\n\nSo your concealed hand must form two more melds.\n\nWith 8 tiles, that\u2019s possible if you have two chows and a pair, for example.\n\nSuppose you have:\n- B6,B7,B8 in hand \u2192 chow\n- F3,F3 \u2192 pair\n- W6,W9,F1 \u2014 need one more tile to complete a meld.\n\nNot possible.\n\nUnless you have another pung.\n\nYou have only one of each except F3.\n\nSo best bet: you are waiting on a tile to complete a pair or a chow.\n\nLook at your hand: W6, W9, B6, B7, B8, F1, F3\u00d72\n\nAfter removing the two known melds, your concealed hand is these 8 tiles.\n\nYou need to form two melds and a pair from these 8.\n\nPossible combinations:\n- Use B6,B7,B8 as a chow\n- Use F3,F3 as pair\n- Left with W6, W9, F1 \u2014 cannot form a meld\n\n- Use W6 and ? \u2014 no\n- Or wait on F3 to become a pung \u2014 but you have only two, and no third F3 available? Unshown: F3:3, so one more exists.\n\nF3: 4 total\n- You have 2\n- Previous player played F3\n- So one used, you have two, so one left.\n\nSo possible to draw F3 to make pung.\n\nThen you would have:\n- Melds: [B5\u00d73], [B6,B7,B8], and new [F3\u00d73]\n- Need one more meld and a pair from W6, W9, B6, B7, B8, F1\n\nStill messy.\n\nPerhaps the [B6,B7,B8] in melds is the only one, and the B6, B7, B8 in hand are not usable for another chow because of lack of sequence.\n\nAnother idea: perhaps you are aiming for **All Even Pungs** or something.\n\nBut let's look at patterns.\n\nYou have [B5\u00d73] \u2014 pung of even number? B5 is odd.\n\nB5 is 5, odd.\n\nW6 is even, etc.\n\nPerhaps you are waiting on a tile to complete a chow like W5,W6,W7 \u2014 but W5 has 2 unshown, W6 has 0 unshown (all four copies are out), so W6 is safe to discard.\n\nW6: unshown 0 -> all four copies are exposed.\n\nWhere are they?\n- You have one in hand\n- Previous player: has [W4,W5,W6] meld -> one W6\n- Across: played W6\n- Next: played W6? History: Play W7, Play W9, Play W5, Play W4 \u2014 no W6\n- You: one in hand\n- Previous: one in meld\n- Across: one played\n- That's three. Fourth must be in wall or hand.\n\nUnshown: W6:0 -> so none left -> fourth is either in hand or about to be drawn.\n\nSo W6 is completely safe to discard \u2014 no one can use it.\n\nSimilarly, T9: unshown 0, F2:0, F4:0, B3:0 \u2014 all gone.\n\nSo **Play W6** is very safe.\n\nSimilarly, W9: unshown 3, so some left.\n\nF1: unshown 4, so all copies available \u2014 risky to discard.\n\nF3: unshown 3, you have two, one played by previous, so one left \u2014 medium risk.\n\nB6, B7, B8: various levels.\n\nBut among valid actions, **Play W6** stands out as safest because W6 is fully exhausted.\n\nMoreover, you have no use for it \u2014 you have no W5 or W7 to form a chow (W7 has 0 unshown, but you don't have W5 or W8).\n\nW5: unshown 2, but you don't have W4 or W6 to complete chow easily.\n\nAnd you have only one W6, so not useful for pair.\n\nSo no value in keeping W6.\n\nTherefore, best move is to discard W6.\n\nAnswer: Play W6\n\nEvaluation: Among the valid discard options, W6 is the safest because all four copies of W6 have been revealed (through discards or melds), making it impossible for any opponent to use it. This satisfies the \"Last Tile\" pattern for others, but since it's gone, no one can win on it. Other tiles like F1, F3, or B6/B7/B8 still have unseen copies and could be dangerous. W6 provides no value to our hand as we lack connecting tiles (W5, W7) to form chows, and we already have a pair of F3. Thus, discarding W6 minimizes risk.\n\nReason: W6 is completely safe to discard as it has been exhausted from the wall, and it offers no strategic value to our hand.\n\nAnswer: Play W6"}
