Still, better to keep it as potential scoring tile.\n\nRe-evaluating hand:\n- Your melds: [B6,B7,B8] (Chow), [B5,B5,B5] (Pung).\n- Hand: T1, T2, T6, B6, B7, F1, F3, F3 \u2014 but B6 and B7 are used in a meld, so actual hand should only contain non-meld tiles. Assuming \"hand tiles\" means current live hand after melds, perhaps there's duplication. Likely confusion between melded and held tiles.\n\nBut based on stated hand: [T1:1, T2:1, T6:1, B6:1, B7:1, F1:1, F3:2] \u2192 total 7 tiles. Melds account for 6 tiles (3+3), so 13 tiles accounted for? No\u2014player holds 13 before draw, 14 after. You haven\u2019t drawn yet\u2014so likely about to discard after drawing.\n\nWait: valid actions include **Play X**, meaning you\u2019ve drawn a tile and now must discard one.\n\nSo current hand (before discard): T1, T2, T6, B6, B7, F1, F3, F3 \u2192 8 tiles? Contradiction.\n\nAh, likely: \u201cYour hand tiles\u201d means current live hand (after drawing), so 14 tiles including the drawn one.\n\nYou have two F3s, and melds use B5x3, B6/B7/B8 \u2014 so B6 and B7 in hand must be duplicates? But only 4 copies exist.\n\nB6: you have one in hand, one in meld \u2192 okay (two total). B7: one in meld, one in hand \u2192 but B7 count: unshown is 2, and you have two \u2192 possible.\n\nBut meld [B6,B7,B8] uses one of each. So you can still hold extra B6/B7.\n\nHowever, **you cannot have B6 or B7 in hand and also in a meld unless duplicated** \u2014 which is allowed.\n\nSo possible you have:  \n- Meld: [B6,B7,B8] (exposed)  \n- Also hold: B6, B7 \u2192 so you have two of each.\n\nBut unshown B6: 3 remain, you have at least one \u2192 okay. Unshown B7: 2 remain, you have one in meld and possibly one in hand \u2192 total 2 \u2192 so yes, possible.\n\nBut then playing B6 or B7 would break potential pair or future kong? Not useful.\n\nBack to evaluation:\n\nBest move is to discard safely while avoiding giving opponents scoring tiles.\n\nKey observations:\n- F2 (South Wind) and F4 (North Wind)