 Wind** (2 points) if you get third.\n\nSimilarly, F1 could contribute to **Prevalent Wind** if someone else uses it.\n\nBut you want to win.\n\nCurrent hand structure: too scattered for high-scoring hand.\n\nLow-tier patterns possible:\n- **Outside Hand**? Requires every meld to have terminal or honor. Your [B6,B7,B8] \u2014 6,7,8 are simples, not terminals \u2192 fails.\n- **All Chows**? You have a pung \u2192 fails.\n- **All Pungs**? You have a chow \u2192 fails.\n- **Half Flush**? You have T1,T2,T6 \u2014 bamboo \u2014 and B and F \u2192 mixed \u2192 no.\n- Best bet: **Chicken Hand** (8 points minimum) \u2014 i.e., just scrape by with whatever.\n\nBut need at least 8 points.\n\nPossible contributors:\n- **Two Concealed Pungs**: You have none concealed \u2014 both melds are exposed (from Peng/Chi).\n- **Mixed Double Chow**: do you have two identical chows in different suits? No.\n- **Short Straight**: two consecutive chows? No.\n- Only possible points:\n    - If you complete a pung of F3 \u2192 **Seat Wind** (2 points)\n    - F1 \u2192 **Prevalent Wind** (2 points) \u2014 but only if you make pung\n    - Otherwise, maybe **One Voided Suit**? You have B and T \u2014 two suits \u2014 and honors. Missing Characters (W) \u2192 yes! You have no W tiles except possibly in melds? Melds: [B6,B7,B8], [B5,B5,B5] \u2014 both B. Hand: T1,T2,T6,B6,B7,F1,F3,F3 \u2014 so suits: B and T, and honors. No W tiles \u2192 so **One Voided Suit** (1 point) applies.\n    - **No Honors**? No \u2014 you have many \u2192 doesn't apply.\n    - **Self Drawn**? Unknown \u2014 depends on how you win.\n    - **All Simples**? No \u2014 you have terminals (T1) and honors.\n\nBut currently, no clear path.\n\nBack to discard choice.\n\nSafe discards: T1, T2, T6 \u2014 all seem safe.\n\nT6: less likely to complete 456 or 567, since T5 is low circulation, T7/T8 all discarded.\n\nT1: could start 123, but T3 is scarce.\n\nBut **the next player has meld [W1,W2,W3]** \u2014 not bamboo.\n\nThe across player also has same.\n\nPrevious player has [B3,B3,B3], [F2,F2,F2], [W4,W5,W6] \u2014 so no bamboo chows.\n\nNo evidence anyone is collecting bamboo.\n\nSo T1, T2, T6 all safe.\n\nBut among them, T6 is highest \u2014 more likely to be needed for 567 or 678? But T7/T8 gone.\n\nSo T6 safest.\n\nBut let\u2019s check unshown counts:\n- T1: 1 unshown \u2014 you don\u2019t know who has it\n- T2: 1 unshown\n- T6: 2 unshown\n\nSo T1 and T2 are rarer \u2014 slightly riskier.\n\nT6: two left \u2014 safer to discard.\n\nF3: you have two \u2014 don\u2019t discard \u2014 potential seat wind pung.\n\nF1: single copy \u2014 discard risks giving opponent a chance at prevalent wind.\n\nSo best to discard a bamboo tile.\n\nBetween T1, T2, T6: T6 is safest due to higher unshown count and lower utility in common hands.\n\nBut wait: you already have T1 and T2 \u2014 discarding one breaks potential 123 chow, but without T3, useless.\n\nSame for both.\n\nActually, **T6** is better to keep than T1/T2? Why?\n\nBecause T6 could pair with T5/T7 for 567, but T7 is exhausted \u2014 all 4 discarded (you played T7 twice, others played).\n\nSo no T7 left \u2192 567 impossible.\n\nSimilarly, 678 impossible.\n\nSo T6 is useless.\n\nLikewise, T1: 123 possible only if T3 exists \u2014 one T3 unshown \u2014 very unlikely.\n\nSo all three are safe.\n\nBut **T1** is a terminal \u2014 more valuable for patterns like Outside Hand or Terminal Chow.\n\nBut no one seems to be building that.\n\nGiven equal safety, discard **T1** \u2014 it's the most terminal-like, but since no one cares, fine.\n\nWait \u2014 valid actions include Play T1, T2, T6.\n\nBut earlier thought: B6 and B7 should not be playable \u2014 they are part of a meld.\n\nUnless the game system allows it, but in standard rules, once melded, you can't discard from meld.\n\nSo likely, the \"hand tiles\" include tiles not in melds.\n\nPerhaps the meld [B6,B7,B8] is not using the B6/B7 in hand \u2014 duplicate tiles.\n\nBut you can still discard them.\n\nBut it's inefficient to discard B6/B7 \u2014 they could help form a kong or pair.\n\nFor example, if you have two B6s (one in meld, one in hand), you could promote to kong later.\n\nSame for B7.\n\nSo better not to discard B6 or B7.\n\nF3: you have two \u2014 good to keep for seat wind.\n\nF1: keep for prevalent wind potential.\n\nSo only safe discards are T1, T2, T6.\n\nChoose one.\n\nSince you previously played T7, T8, T9, T4, etc., indicating you were dumping bamboo, continue with **T1**.\n\nBut T1 is earlier \u2014 maybe you're saving T6?\n\nNo strategic advantage.\n\nAlternatively, **T1** might be slightly riskier because 1-2-3 is a common beginner pattern.\n\nBut no one has claimed T1 or T2 so far.\n\nIn fact, across player played T1 twice \u2014 and no one chi'd \u2014 so T1 and T2 are likely safe.\n\nSimilarly, next player played T1, T2 \u2014 no chi.\n\nSo all safe.\n\nI think **T6** is the best to discard because:\n- It's in the middle, less likely to be part of edge chows\n- 567 requires T5/T7 \u2014 T7 all gone, so useless\n- T1/T2 could still be collected by someone hoarding, but unlikely\n\nSo **Play T6**\n\nBut let's see: unshown T6: 2 \u2014 so not completely dead.\n\nStill, safest.\n\nAlternatively, **Play T1** \u2014 same.\n\nBut T6 is higher frequency, so better to keep? No.\n\nFinal decision: discard **T6**.\n\nReason: T6 is a middle bamboo tile with limited value, as T7 and T8 are fully discarded, eliminating common chows (567, 678). The remaining T6 tiles are less likely to complete opponents' hands compared to T1 or T2, which could theoretically start a 123 sequence (though unlikely). Keeping F1 and F3 supports potential wind-based scoring patterns (Prevalent and Seat Wind), while B6 and B7 could be used to upgrade to a kong. Among the viable discards, T6 poses the least risk.\n\nAnswer: Play T6"}
